Task: Load ClientResilienceExampleSuite's config via PureConfig (follow-up)

Summary:
User asked for the resilience example to be extended: add a named "dummy
client" config block to application.conf containing retry + circuit-breaker
settings (clearly commented as example-only, not read by the live service),
and have the test load it via PureConfig rather than hardcoding
ResilienceConfig literals.

Added example-client { retry {...} circuit-breaker {...} } to
application.conf, matching the exact values the first test previously
hardcoded (max-retries=5, base-delay=1ms, failure-threshold=10,
reset-timeout=1h). ClientResilienceExampleSuite now derives ConfigReader
instances for purerest's RetryConfig/CircuitBreakerConfig/ResilienceConfig
(purerest has no PureConfig dependency itself, so this is done locally - the
same pattern the original order-service's OrderServiceConfig.scala used
before the Phase 1 deviation removed it) and loads
ConfigSource.default.at("example-client").loadOrThrow[ResilienceConfig] once
as a val, used directly in the first test (retry-succeeds) and as a base,
.copy()'d for the fields that need isolating, in the second (circuit-breaker
trips) - since that test's precise assertions (attempts==2) depend on
maxRetries=0, which the shared retry-demo config can't also satisfy.

Files changed:
- src/main/resources/application.conf
- src/test/scala/orderservice/examples/ClientResilienceExampleSuite.scala

Why: Makes the example demonstrate the full realistic pattern a generated
service's Main.scala would follow (load config, then wire the client) rather
than just the middleware call with inline literals.

Verification: sbt scalafmtCheck Test/scalafmtCheck test - 52/52 pass.
